  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n  We address the problem of finding a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral (Erds constant) by tackling its non-convex nature and high dimensionality (n=180). The current state-of-the-art is 0.3808, and we aim for a lower value. Our approach leverages distributed computing to explore the search space more effectively than sequential methods, using tailored local optimization around distributed points. We partition the domain into chips and optimize locally, then combine them heuristically. This balances exploration and exploitation, leveraging the high dimensionality where parallel evaluations are feasible.\n\n  Mathematical specification\n  \\subsection{Variables}\n  - $h$: A step function with $\\sum h_i \\Delta x = 1$ and $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ for all $i$.\n  - $\\Delta x = \\frac{2}{n}$, $n=180$.\n  - Objective: $\\min \\max_l \\sum_i h_i (1-h_{i+l}) \\Delta x$.\n\n  \\subsection{Method}\n  Use distributed parallel optimization via interval partitioning (chipping) and combine with linear interpolation for cross-chip correlations. For each chip:\n  - Express constraints via quadratic penalties.\n  - Optimize using CVXOPT or ECOS solvers.\n  - Combine via eigenvector-like interpolation under linear correlation constraints.\n\n  \\subsection{Representation}\n  Represent $h$ as a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\\sum h_i = \\frac{n}{2}$ and $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$.\n\n  \\subsection{Constraints}\n  We handle bounds and integral constraint via projected gradient steps with domain transformation.\n\n  Implementation procedure\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Initialization}\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n      \\item \\textit{Generate} $k$ points in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as reference chips (split domain).\n      \\item \\textit{For each chip}, optimize locally subject to $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ and $\\sum h_i = \\frac{n}{2}$ via CVXOPT (QP) or IPOPT.\n      \\item \\textit{Take} the best candidate as initial solution. \n    \\end{enumerate}\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Parallel Optimization}\n      \\begin{enumerate}\n        \\item \\textit{Run} a parallel SPOT (Sequential Parameter Optimization) algorithm using \\emph{joblib} to tune hyperparameters for the local optimization.\n        \\item \\textit{Sweep} several hyperparameters ($\\lambda=10^{-k}$) and model the objective via Gaussian process regression.\n        \\item \\textit{For each candidate point}, use local optimization while tracking runs.\n      \\end{enumerate}\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Cross-chip Correlation}\n      \\begin{enumerate}\n        \\item \\textit{Interpolate} the chips using an eigenvector that minimizes the maximum correlation between interpolated functions.\n        \\item \\textit{Compute} \\emph{global} correlation to ensure the interpolated function is optimal.\n      \\end{enumerate}\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Termination}\n      \\begin{enumerate}\n        \\item \\textit{Stop} when time exceeds or max iterations $>500$, or variable selection converges.\n      \\end{enumerate}\n\n  \\subsection{Algorithm-Specific Parameters}\n  - Number of chips: $m=15$\n  - Time threshold for split: $100$ seconds.\n  - Local optimization parameters: $\\lambda \\in \\{1e-4, 1e-5, 1e-6\\}$.\n  - Use \\emph{CVXOPT} for QP subproblems and \\emph{ECOS} for small-scale linear QPs.\n\n  Search choices and useful variations\n  \\subsection{Initial Points}\n  Choose $m$ uniformly distributed initial splits of $[0,2]$ into $n$ intervals.\n\n  \\subsection{Optimization Strategies}\n  - Robust vs. Aggressive: For early iterations, balance exploration ($\\lambda$ small) vs. exploitation ($\\lambda$ large).\n  - Linear vs. Quadratic: CD: User chooses solver, retraining automatically.\n\n  Computebudget and robustness\n  \\subsection{Time Estimation}\n  - Parallel evaluation of $15$ chips: $15 \\times 1 = 15$ calls per batch with parameter tuning taking $\\sim3-4$ evaluations to converge.\n  - Total budget: $200$ s for evaluation, $\\sim100$ s for final run.\n\n  \\subsection{Fail-safe}\n  - Early fallback: Optimize over single chip $h$ without domain partitioning.\n\n  \\subsection{Validation}\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item After main run, recompute final $h$ and \\( C_5 \\) using the provided function to ensure compatibility with evaluator.\n    \\item Check sum $\\sum h_i = \\frac{n}{2}$ and $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ for feasibility.\n    \\item Verify that the returned $h$ has valid entries and is a \\textit{step function}.\n\n  \\end{enumerate}",
